{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2145-04-10 23:41:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee, one of the hospital doctors. You're here because of severe stomach pain and vomiting again. Can you tell me what’s been going on?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2145-04-10 23:42:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, doctor. The pain came back just two days after I got home. It's the same burning, bloating pain in my upper belly. I can’t keep anything down — not even water. I’ve thrown up four times."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2145-04-10 23:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. That sounds like your gastroparesis acting up again. You were just admitted for this last week. Are you still on your usual medications?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2145-04-10 23:45:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I’m on metoclopramide, tramadol, and insulin. But I only took 4 units today because I only had broth. My blood sugars have been in the 100s to 200s."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2145-04-10 23:46:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay, we’ve started you on IV fluids and gave you some anti-nausea and pain meds in the ER. We’ll keep monitoring your pain and try to get you comfortable. We also checked your blood work."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2145-04-10 23:48:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. But honestly, I’m tired of just getting meds and sent home. I need something that actually helps long-term."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2145-04-10 23:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I hear you. Your diabetes is a big part of what’s driving the gastroparesis. Your A1c came back at 8.3%, which means your sugars haven’t been well controlled. That makes the stomach emptying worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2145-04-10 23:51:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I try, but it’s hard. The pain makes me not want to eat, and when I do, I throw it up. It’s a cycle."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2145-04-10 23:53:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Absolutely. That’s why the best long-term fix is tighter sugar control and eating small, frequent meals. We’ve added ondansetron for nausea, and we’ll keep you on your regular metoclopramide."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2145-04-11 06:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your pain seems a bit better, and your blood pressure has come down from 188 to 165. It was likely high because of the pain. We didn’t change your blood pressure meds — just focused on treating the flare."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2145-04-11 06:17:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. My stomach feels less tight. Can I try eating something?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2145-04-11 06:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’d like you to try a small meal — maybe some toast and applesauce. If you keep that down, we’ll advance your diet. That’s a good sign you’re improving."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2145-04-11 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You did well with breakfast. You’re tolerating food, and your blood sugar this morning was 142. We’ll have you resume your full insulin dose when you go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2145-04-11 08:32:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. What about those blood tests? I saw my white count was high."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2145-04-11 08:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your white count and platelets have been high for a while, even when you’re not sick. We don’t see an infection now, but we recommend you follow up with your doctor to check it again. Maybe see a hematologist if it stays up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2145-04-11 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve had a full meal and no vomiting. We’re planning to discharge you today. We’ll send you home with ondansetron for nausea and instructions to keep taking your regular meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2145-04-11 10:02:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. And I’m switching PCPs — I already have an appointment scheduled. I just want someone who listens."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2145-04-11 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s completely valid. We’ll make sure your records are sent. Remember: control your diabetes, eat small meals, and call if the pain returns or you can’t keep food down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2145-04-11 12:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just a quick check before you leave. You’re still feeling okay after lunch?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2145-04-11 12:16:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I ate most of my sandwich and haven’t felt sick. I’m ready to go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2145-04-11 12:18:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. We’ll get your discharge papers ready. Take it slow, stick to small meals, and follow up with your new doctor soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2145-04-11 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Here’s your prescription for ondansetron — take it only if you feel nauseous. And keep using your metoclopramide, though we recommend discussing long-term use with your provider due to possible side effects."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2145-04-11 14:02:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. No more big meals, better sugar control, and small steps. Thank you for actually talking to me."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2145-04-11 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. You did well today. We’ll see you only if you need us — otherwise, take care and keep in touch with your care team."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2145-04-11 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just confirming — you’re feeling stable, no nausea or pain right now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2145-04-11 15:01:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, I feel much better. Still uncomfortable, but nothing like last night."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2145-04-11 15:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s progress. We’ll discharge you shortly. Remember to check your blood pressure and sugars regularly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2145-04-11 16:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "All set. You’re cleared to go home. Rest well, and we hope not to see you back here soon."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2145-04-10 23:41:00",
    "admission_end": "2145-04-11 16:30:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 46-year-old woman with a history of type 2 diabetes, gastroparesis, and obesity was admitted for acute-on-chronic epigastric pain and vomiting due to a gastroparesis flare, just five days after a prior discharge. She was treated with IV fluids, ondansetron, metoclopramide, and pain control with acetaminophen and tramadol. Her blood pressure was elevated likely due to pain, and her A1c was 8.3%, indicating suboptimal diabetes control. She gradually improved, tolerated a full diet, and was discharged home with instructions to maintain strict glucose control, eat small frequent meals, and follow up for persistent leukocytosis. No surgical procedures were performed.",
    "problems": [
      "Gastroparesis",
      "Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus",
      "Hypertension",
      "Leukocytosis",
      "Obesity",
      "Poorly controlled diabetes (A1c 8.3%)",
      "Chronic abdominal pain",
      "Recurrent nausea and vomiting"
    ]
  }
}
